<!-- ═══════════════════ CHAPTER 4 ═══════════════════ -->
<h1>4. System Architecture</h1>

<h2>4.1 Monorepo Structure</h2>
<p>The project follows an npm workspaces monorepo pattern with three workspace packages:</p>

<div class="code-block">fwms/
├── package.json              ← Root orchestrator (workspaces, scripts)
├── apps/
│   ├── api/                  ← @fwms/api — Express.js REST API
│   │   ├── prisma/           ← Database schema, migrations, seed script
│   │   └── src/
│   │       ├── server.ts     ← Entry point
│   │       ├── cron/         ← Background scheduled jobs
│   │       ├── lib/          ← Prisma client, JWT helpers, logger
│   │       ├── middleware/   ← Auth, validation, error handling
│   │       └── modules/      ← Feature-based business logic
│   │           ├── auth/
│   │           ├── institutional/
│   │           ├── faculty/
│   │           ├── subjects/
│   │           ├── classes-batches/
│   │           ├── allocations/
│   │           ├── approvals/
│   │           ├── conflict-engine/
│   │           ├── workload-engine/
│   │           ├── dashboard/
│   │           └── workload-report/
│   └── web/                  ← Next.js 16 Frontend
│       └── src/
│           ├── app/          ← App Router pages and layouts
│           │   ├── login/
│           │   ├── unauthorized/
│           │   └── (protected)/
│           │       ├── dashboard/
│           │       ├── institutional/
│           │       ├── master-data/
│           │       ├── subjects/
│           │       ├── allocations/
│           │       ├── approvals/
│           │       ├── faculty/
│           │       └── workload-report/
│           ├── components/   ← Reusable UI components
│           ├── lib/          ← API HTTP client
│           └── stores/       ← Zustand auth store
└── packages/
    └── shared/               ← @fwms/shared — Zod schemas, enums, types</div>

<h2>4.2 Backend Architecture</h2>
<p>The API follows a <b>modular service-oriented architecture</b>. Each feature domain is organised into a self-contained module with:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>routes.ts</b> — Express Router defining HTTP endpoints, applying middleware, and delegating to the service layer</li>
  <li><b>service.ts</b> — Pure business logic operating on Prisma models, performing computations, and enforcing invariants</li>
</ul>

<p>Cross-cutting concerns are handled by centralised middleware:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Middleware</th><th>File</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>middleware/auth.ts</td><td>JWT verification, role gating, department scoping, self-or-admin checks</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Validation</td><td>middleware/validation.ts</td><td>Zod schema validation for request body and query parameters</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Error Handling</td><td>middleware/error-handler.ts</td><td>Custom ApiError class, centralized JSON error responses, stack trace suppression in production</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.3 Frontend Architecture</h2>
<p>The web application uses <b>Next.js 16 App Router</b> with the following patterns:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Route Groups:</b> A <code>(protected)</code> route group wraps all authenticated pages inside a <code>RouteGuard</code> component and a shared sidebar layout.</li>
  <li><b>Client Components:</b> Dashboard charts, forms, and interactive tables are <code>'use client'</code> components using React hooks and Recharts.</li>
  <li><b>API Client:</b> A custom <code>apiClient</code> function handles all HTTP requests, automatically attaching Bearer tokens, intercepting 401 responses, and performing silent token refresh.</li>
  <li><b>State Management:</b> Zustand with <code>persist</code> middleware stores the authenticated user profile in localStorage, while the access token is kept only in memory.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.4 Shared Package (<code>@fwms/shared</code>)</h2>
<p>Contains all Zod validation schemas and TypeScript types shared between frontend and backend. This ensures:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Identical validation rules on client and server</li>
  <li>Automatic TypeScript type inference from Zod schemas (e.g., <code>CreateFacultyRequest</code>)</li>
  <li>Mirrored enums that decouple the frontend from the Prisma dependency</li>
</ul>

<!-- ═══════════════════ CHAPTER 5 ═══════════════════ -->
<h1>5. Database Design</h1>

<h2>5.1 Entity-Relationship Overview</h2>
<p>The database contains <b>14 tables</b> managed through Prisma ORM on SQLite. The core relationships are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>School → Department</b> (1:N) — Schools group departments</li>
  <li><b>Department → Faculty</b> (1:N) — Departments employ faculty</li>
  <li><b>Faculty → UserAuth</b> (1:1) — Each faculty member has login credentials</li>
  <li><b>Department → Subject</b> (1:N) — Departments offer subjects per term</li>
  <li><b>Department → ClassDivision</b> (1:N) — Departments define class sections</li>
  <li><b>ClassDivision → Batch</b> (1:N) — Classes split into practical batches</li>
  <li><b>Faculty + Subject + Class/Batch → Allocation</b> — The central junction record</li>
  <li><b>Allocation → ApprovalLog</b> (1:N) — Full audit trail per allocation</li>
  <li><b>AcademicTerm → Norms</b> (1:N) — Term-specific workload limits per designation</li>
</ul>

<h2>5.2 Model Descriptions</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Model</th><th>DB Table</th><th>Description</th><th>Key Fields</th></tr>
  <tr><td>School</td><td>schools</td><td>Top-level institutional unit (faculty/school)</td><td>schoolName, deanName, isActive</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Department</td><td>departments</td><td>Academic department under a school</td><td>deptName, schoolId, hodId, defaultBatchSize</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AcademicTerm</td><td>academic_terms</td><td>Semester/year configuration</td><td>academicYear, semester, status (active/archived), startDate, endDate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Faculty</td><td>faculty</td><td>Teaching staff master record</td><td>name, email (unique), designation, deptId, employmentType, status</td></tr>
  <tr><td>UserAuth</td><td>user_auth</td><td>Login credentials &amp; security</td><td>username, passwordHash, role, linkedId (→Faculty), tokenVersion, failedLoginAttempts, lockedUntil, mustChangePassword</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Subject</td><td>subjects</td><td>Course offerings per department per term</td><td>subjectCode, subjectName, creditHours, theoryMultiplier, practicalMultiplier, tutorialHours, hasTheory/hasPractical/hasTutorial</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Norms</td><td>norms</td><td>Workload limits per designation per term</td><td>designation, minWeeklyHours, maxWeeklyHours, defaultTheoryMultiplier, defaultPracticalMultiplier, slaDaysForHodReview</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ClassDivision</td><td>class_divisions</td><td>Section/division of a student class</td><td>className, division, deptId, termId</td></tr>
  <tr><td>StudentStrength</td><td>student_strengths</td><td>Student count per class per subject</td><td>classId, subjectId, studentCount, status</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Batch</td><td>batches</td><td>Practical sub-groups</td><td>classId, subjectId, batchName, batchSize, facultyId</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Allocation</td><td>allocations</td><td>Faculty-Subject-Class/Batch assignment</td><td>facultyId, subjectId, classId, batchId, termId, theoryHours, practicalHours, totalHours, status, requiresSaOverride</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ApprovalLog</td><td>approval_logs</td><td>Status transition audit trail</td><td>entityType, entityId, requestedBy, reviewedBy, fromStatus, toStatus, changeType, remarks, slaEscalated</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Notification</td><td>notifications</td><td>In-app user notifications</td><td>userId, message, type, read</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AuditLog</td><td>audit_logs</td><td>Security event log</td><td>userId, action, ipAddress, metadata (JSON)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>WorkloadReport</td><td>workload_reports</td><td>Department workload report snapshot</td><td>deptId, termId, title, notes</td></tr>
  <tr><td>WorkloadReportRow</td><td>workload_report_rows</td><td>Individual row in a workload report</td><td>programClass, subjectName, divisions, theory/practical/tutorial hours, credits (L/P/T), noteMarker</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.3 Enumerations</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Enum</th><th>Values</th><th>Used In</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Role</td><td>super_admin, dept_admin, faculty</td><td>UserAuth.role</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Designation</td><td>Professor, Associate Professor, Assistant Professor</td><td>Faculty.designation, Norms.designation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>EmploymentType</td><td>Permanent, Contract, Visiting</td><td>Faculty.employmentType</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AccountStatus</td><td>active, inactive</td><td>UserAuth.status, Faculty.status</td></tr>
  <tr><td>TermStatus</td><td>active, archived</td><td>AcademicTerm.status</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AllocationStatus</td><td>draft, pending_approval, approved, rejected, escalated_to_super_admin</td><td>Allocation.status, StudentStrength.status</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ApprovalEntityType</td><td>allocation, student_strength, batch_change</td><td>ApprovalLog.entityType</td></tr>
  <tr><td>NotificationType</td><td>approval_update, sla_escalation, account_status, allocation_override</td><td>Notification.type</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AuditAction</td><td>login_success, login_failed, logout, password_reset_requested, password_changed, role_changed, conflict_override, norms_changed, record_created, etc.</td><td>AuditLog.action</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ═══════════════════ CHAPTER 6 ═══════════════════ -->
<h1>6. Authentication &amp; Security</h1>

<h2>6.1 JWT Token Architecture</h2>
<p>The system implements a <b>dual-token architecture</b> for session management:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Token</th><th>Type</th><th>Expiry</th><th>Storage</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Access Token</td><td>JWT</td><td>15 minutes</td><td>In-memory (JavaScript variable)</td><td>Authorises individual API requests via <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Refresh Token</td><td>JWT</td><td>7 days</td><td>httpOnly, sameSite:strict cookie (path: /api/v1/auth)</td><td>Issues new access tokens without re-login; cannot be read by JavaScript (XSS-safe)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>JWT Payload Structure</h3>
<div class="code-block">{
  userId: number,
  role: 'super_admin' | 'dept_admin' | 'faculty',
  deptId: number | null,
  facultyId: number | null,
  tokenVersion: number
}</div>

<h3>Token Revocation via Version Tracking</h3>
<p>Each user has a <code>tokenVersion</code> counter in the database. On logout or password change, this counter is incremented. During refresh, if the token's version does not match the database version, the refresh is rejected — effectively revoking all existing sessions.</p>

<h3>Silent Token Refresh (Client-Side)</h3>
<p>The frontend <code>apiClient</code> intercepts 401 responses. It queues pending requests, issues a single <code>POST /auth/refresh</code>, receives a new access token, then replays all queued requests. This provides seamless session continuity.</p>

<h2>6.2 Account Lockout &amp; Brute Force Protection</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Max Failed Attempts:</b> 5 consecutive invalid passwords</li>
  <li><b>Lockout Duration:</b> 15 minutes (auto-unlock)</li>
  <li><b>Rate Limiting:</b> Auth endpoints limited to 10 requests/minute; general API limited to 100 requests/minute</li>
  <li><b>Security Headers:</b> Helmet middleware sets CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, etc.</li>
  <li><b>Audit Logging:</b> Every login attempt (success/fail), logout, password change, and lockout event is recorded with IP address and metadata</li>
</ul>

<h2>6.3 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</h2>
<p>Four middleware functions enforce access control:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Middleware</th><th>Function</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>authenticate</code></td><td>Verifies the JWT access token and attaches <code>req.user</code> with role, department, and faculty IDs</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>requireRole(...roles)</code></td><td>Restricts endpoint access to specified roles (returns 403 for others)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>requireDeptScope</code></td><td>HODs can only access their own department's data; Super Admin bypasses</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>requireSelfOrAdmin</code></td><td>Faculty can only view their own profile; HODs and SA can view any faculty</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6.4 Forced Password Change</h2>
<p>New accounts are created with <code>mustChangePassword = true</code>. On first login, the frontend <code>RouteGuard</code> component intercepts the session and displays a mandatory password change form. Until completed, the user cannot access any protected pages. After changing, the old sessions are invalidated via token version increment.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════ CHAPTER 7 ═══════════════════ -->
<h1>7. Feature Catalogue</h1>

<h2>7.1 Institutional Configuration</h2>
<p><b>Accessible by:</b> Super Admin</p>
<p>Complete CRUD management for the institutional hierarchy:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Schools:</b> Create, update, soft-delete (deactivate) institutional units</li>
  <li><b>Departments:</b> Create under schools, assign HOD (auto-promotes the faculty user to <code>dept_admin</code> role, auto-demotes the previous HOD to <code>faculty</code>), set default batch sizes</li>
  <li><b>Academic Terms:</b> Define academic year, semester name, start/end dates with active/archived status management</li>
  <li><b>Workload Norms:</b> Configure per-designation (Professor, Associate Professor, Assistant Professor) per-term: minimum weekly hours, maximum weekly hours, default theory/practical multipliers, and HOD review SLA days. Uses <code>upsert</code> to create-or-update.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.2 Faculty Management</h2>
<p><b>Accessible by:</b> Super Admin, HOD</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Create Faculty:</b> Registers a new faculty member and simultaneously creates their <code>UserAuth</code> login record in a database transaction. Generates a temporary password if none is provided. Optionally designates the faculty as HOD.</li>
  <li><b>Update Faculty:</b> Modify name, designation, employment type, and HOD status. HOD promotion/demotion is transactional — the previous HOD's role is automatically reverted.</li>
  <li><b>Deactivate/Activate:</b> Soft-disable faculty accounts by setting <code>status = inactive</code> on both Faculty and UserAuth records, preventing login. Reactivation reverses this.</li>
  <li><b>Temporary Password Generation:</b> A 10-character alphanumeric string excluding ambiguous characters (0, O, I, l).</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.3 Subject &amp; Class Management</h2>
<p><b>Accessible by:</b> Super Admin, HOD</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Subjects:</b> Full CRUD with fields for subject code, name, credit hours, component flags (theory/practical/tutorial), and per-subject multipliers. Unique constraint on <code>(subjectCode, termId)</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Class Divisions:</b> Define sections (e.g., "TE (I) CE - Div A") per department per term. Unique on <code>(className, division, deptId, termId)</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Batches:</b> Create practical sub-groups under a class, specifying batch name, size, and assigned faculty.</li>
  <li><b>Student Strength:</b> Record student counts per class-subject combination, subject to approval workflow.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.4 Workload Calculation Engine</h2>
<p><b>Module:</b> <code>workload-engine/service.ts</code></p>
<p>This engine computes the teaching hours for each allocation based on subject configuration:</p>

<div class="callout">
  <p class="callout-title">Calculation Formulas</p>
  <p><b>Theory Hours</b> = Credit Hours × Theory Multiplier (when allocation is at class level, no batch)</p>
  <p><b>Practical Hours</b> = Credit Hours × Practical Multiplier × Number of Batches (when allocation has a batch)</p>
  <p><b>Tutorial Hours</b> = Tutorial Hours per Week × Batch Count (or direct if class-level)</p>
  <p><b>Total Weekly Hours</b> = Theory + Practical + Tutorial</p>
</div>

<p>The engine also supports <b>bulk recalculation</b>: when a subject's multipliers are updated, all linked allocations across the system are automatically recomputed.</p>

<h2>7.5 Conflict Detection Engine</h2>
<p><b>Module:</b> <code>conflict-engine/service.ts</code></p>
<p>This engine enforces two constraints during every allocation create/update:</p>

<h3>7.5.1 Double-Booking Check (Hard Constraint)</h3>
<p>Queries existing approved or pending allocations for the same <code>(subjectId, termId, classId/batchId)</code>. If another faculty is already assigned, the system throws a <b>409 Conflict</b> error with the name of the conflicting faculty member. This is a blocking constraint — the allocation cannot proceed.</p>

<h3>7.5.2 Workload Norms Check (Soft Constraint)</h3>
<p>Computes the faculty's projected total weekly hours by summing all existing allocations plus the new one. Compares against the <code>Norms</code> table for the faculty's designation and term:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Overloaded:</b> Projected hours &gt; maxWeeklyHours → Allocation is flagged with <code>requiresSaOverride = true</code>. It remains in <code>draft</code> status and can only be approved by a Super Admin.</li>
  <li><b>Underloaded:</b> Projected hours &lt; minWeeklyHours → Informational flag (does not block).</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.6 Allocation Management</h2>
<p><b>Accessible by:</b> Super Admin, HOD, Faculty (read-only)</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Create:</b> Links a faculty member to a subject + class/batch for a term. Automatically computes hours via the Workload Engine, checks conflicts via the Conflict Engine, and creates an ApprovalLog entry.</li>
  <li><b>Update:</b> Modifies assignment details. Re-validates all constraints. Only allowed for <code>draft</code> or <code>returned_for_clarification</code> statuses.</li>
  <li><b>Delete:</b> Only <code>draft</code> allocations can be deleted. Approved allocations must be ended with an <code>effectiveEndDate</code> instead.</li>
  <li><b>List &amp; Filter:</b> Supports filtering by termId, deptId, and facultyId with eager loading of related faculty, subject, class, and batch names.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.7 Approval Workflow</h2>
<p><b>Module:</b> <code>approvals/service.ts</code></p>

<h3>Status Transitions</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Action</th><th>Who Can Do It</th></tr>
  <tr><td>draft</td><td>pending_approval</td><td>Submit for Review</td><td>HOD</td></tr>
  <tr><td>draft (overloaded)</td><td>escalated_to_super_admin</td><td>Submit (auto-escalated)</td><td>HOD</td></tr>
  <tr><td>pending_approval</td><td>approved</td><td>Approve</td><td>HOD or Super Admin</td></tr>
  <tr><td>pending_approval</td><td>rejected</td><td>Reject (remarks ≥ 10 chars required)</td><td>HOD or Super Admin</td></tr>
  <tr><td>pending_approval</td><td>returned_for_clarification</td><td>Return for Clarification</td><td>HOD or Super Admin</td></tr>
  <tr><td>escalated_to_super_admin</td><td>approved</td><td>Override Approve</td><td>Super Admin only</td></tr>
  <tr><td>escalated_to_super_admin</td><td>rejected</td><td>Override Reject</td><td>Super Admin only</td></tr>
  <tr><td>returned_for_clarification</td><td>draft</td><td>Edit and resubmit</td><td>HOD</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Every status change creates an <code>ApprovalLog</code> record with the actor, timestamps, from/to status, change type, and optional remarks.</p>

<h2>7.8 SLA Escalation (Automated Background Job)</h2>
<p><b>Module:</b> <code>cron/sla-escalation.ts</code></p>
<p>A <code>node-cron</code> job runs <b>daily at midnight (0 0 * * *)</b>. For each allocation in <code>pending_approval</code> status:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Finds the most recent ApprovalLog entry where <code>toStatus = 'pending_approval'</code></li>
  <li>Retrieves the SLA days from the Norms table for the faculty's designation (default: 3 days)</li>
  <li>If the pending duration exceeds the SLA, flags the allocation with <code>requiresSaOverride = true</code></li>
  <li>Creates an ApprovalLog entry with <code>changeType = 'sla_escalation'</code> and <code>slaEscalated = true</code></li>
</ol>

<h2>7.9 Role-Specific Dashboards</h2>

<h3>7.9.1 Super Admin Dashboard</h3>
<p><b>KPIs:</b> Total Faculty, Total Departments, Average Workload Hours, Overloaded Faculty Count</p>
<p><b>Charts:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Stacked Bar Chart:</b> Theory vs. Practical hours aggregated by department</li>
  <li><b>Faculty Workload Heatmap:</b> Grid of coloured tiles (green = normal, amber = underloaded, orange = overloaded) representing every faculty member across all departments</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Filters:</b> Designation filter, workload status filter, academic term selector</p>

<h3>7.9.2 HOD Dashboard</h3>
<p><b>KPIs:</b> Department Faculty Count, Average Workload, Overloaded Faculty Count</p>
<p><b>Charts:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Pie/Donut Chart:</b> Subject-wise hour distribution showing which courses consume the most teaching hours</li>
  <li><b>Faculty Cards Grid:</b> Individual cards per faculty showing name, designation, total hours, and a colour-coded progress bar relative to min/max norms</li>
</ul>

<h3>7.9.3 Faculty Dashboard</h3>
<p><b>Workload Gauge:</b> A radial progress chart showing the faculty's total allocated hours against their norm limit, with status-dependent colouring</p>
<p><b>Assigned Subjects:</b> Card grid showing each assigned subject, class/batch name, and weekly hours</p>
<p><b>Recent Request Timeline:</b> Vertical timeline with icons (check for approved, X for rejected, clock for pending) showing the last 5 approval status changes</p>

<h2>7.10 Workload Report Generation</h2>
<p><b>Module:</b> <code>workload-report/service.ts</code></p>
<p>Generates structured departmental workload reports matching the official MGM University Teaching Workload Sheet format. Features:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Auto-Generation:</b> Compiles report rows from existing subjects, classes, batches, and their computed hours</li>
  <li><b>Inline Editing:</b> Full spreadsheet-like editing of report rows with add/delete/reorder capabilities</li>
  <li><b>Auto-Calculated Fields:</b> Total Theory Hours = Divisions × Theory Hrs/Week; Total Practical Hours = Batches × Practical Hrs/Week; Total Teaching Hours = Theory + Practical + Tutorial</li>
  <li><b>Print View:</b> A dedicated print-optimised layout with the institutional header, formatted table, totals row, and signature lines</li>
  <li><b>Note Markers:</b> Support for * (load taken by other dept) and ** (load from other dept) annotations</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.11 AI-Powered Document Ingestion</h2>
<p><b>Module:</b> <code>workload-report/ingest.service.ts</code></p>
<p>Integrates <b>Google Gemini 2.5 Flash</b> for multimodal document extraction:</p>

<h3>Supported Document Types</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Type 1 — Teaching Workload Sheet:</b> Subject-wise table with columns for Sr. No., Program/Class, Subject Name, Divisions, Theory Hours, Practical Hours, Tutorial Hours, Credits (L/P/T)</li>
  <li><b>Type 2 — Teaching Workload Distribution Sheet:</b> Faculty-wise table with assignments, student counts, and grand total hours</li>
</ul>

<h3>Processing Pipeline</h3>
<ol>
  <li>File uploaded via <code>POST /workload-report/ingest</code> (multer memory storage, max 20MB)</li>
  <li>File buffer converted to base64 and sent to Gemini with a 230+ line structured extraction prompt</li>
  <li>Prompt enforces 10 strict extraction rules (e.g., never skip rows, normalize designations, handle special markers)</li>
  <li>Response JSON is cleaned (markdown code fences removed) and parsed into a typed <code>GeminiExtractedPayload</code></li>
  <li>Dry-run mode available for preview before committing to database</li>
  <li>On commit: maps extracted subjects to <code>WorkloadReportRow</code> records and saves via the report service</li>
</ol>

